# The MAINNET estate's env file — the half of the pair `compose/testnet.env` has
# had since the second environment appeared, and mainnet went without.
#
#   docker compose --env-file compose/mainnet.env --env-file compose/estate/tokens.env \
#     -f compose/docker-compose.estate.yml -f compose/docker-compose.release.yml up -d
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHAT ITS ABSENCE COST ───────────────────────────
#
# Mainnet had no env file because it did not appear to need one: `compose/.env`
# is a symlink to `estate/tokens.env`, compose loads `.env` automatically, and
# every default in `docker-compose.estate.yml` is written for a dev laptop. So
# mainnet ran on defaults plus credentials and nobody had to remember anything.
#
# The hostnames were the exception, and they were carried in SHELL STATE ONLY.
# `scripts/estate-up.sh:63-65` and `scripts/release-deploy.sh:92-110` both export
# CF_WEB_APEX / CF_WEB_SUFFIX / CF_SITE_HOST before they call compose, so an
# estate brought up through either script got the right values — and an estate
# touched by ANY OTHER command got `.cloudsforge.localtest.me`.
#
# MEASURED, NOT PREDICTED. On 2026-08-05 `cloudsforge-estate-identity-1` was
# recreated at 09:50, eighty-six minutes after the rest of the estate, by a
# command run from a shell that had none of these three exported. It came back
# with:
#
#   IDENTITY_HANDOFF_ORIGINS=https://cloudsforge.localtest.me, https://hub.cloudsforge.localtest.me, …
#
# while the gateway went on serving `hub.cloudsforge.online`. `beacon` and
# `lantern`, created at 08:24 and never touched since, still held the correct
# `.cloudsforge.online` values — so the estate disagreed with ITSELF, and the
# only container that was wrong was the one that decides whether a user can move
# between surfaces. `POST /auth/handoff` answered 403 to every real origin,
# every surface answered 200, and cross-surface SSO was dead for eleven hours
# with nothing red anywhere.
#
# That is the failure `release-deploy.sh:57-111` was written to prevent, and it
# happened anyway, because that guard runs on the DEPLOY path and this recreate
# did not take it. A value that is only correct when it is remembered is a value
# that is eventually wrong. Committing it is the fix; the guard is the net.
#
# ── THEY MUST MATCH `env/traefik.env` EXACTLY ────────────────────────────────
#
# These three are read by TWO mechanisms that cannot see each other's files:
# COMPOSE interpolates them from HERE into identity's IDENTITY_HANDOFF_ORIGINS,
# `IDENTITY_ACCOUNT_URL`, `BEACON_HANDOFF_ORIGIN`, `FAUCET_CORS_ORIGINS` and
# `LANTERN_RUM_ORIGINS`; TRAEFIK's file provider renders them from
# `env/traefik.env` into every surface router and the CORS allowlist. Drift
# between the two is invisible from outside — the surfaces answer and the
# sign-in fails somewhere else entirely.
#
# `scripts/estate-up.sh` and `scripts/release-deploy.sh` both refuse to start
# when the two files disagree, and `release-deploy.sh` additionally refuses a
# rendered hand-off allowlist that is empty or does not name these hostnames.
CF_WEB_APEX=cloudsforge.online
CF_WEB_SUFFIX=.cloudsforge.online
CF_SITE_HOST=cloudsforge.online

# The gateway's env file, named the same way `testnet.env` names its own. The
# default in both scripts is already `traefik`, so this is a statement of the
# obvious ON PURPOSE: the testnet file sets `CF_TRAEFIK_ENV=traefik.testnet`,
# and a pair where only one member declares the variable is a pair where the
# reader has to know which default applies.
CF_TRAEFIK_ENV=traefik

# ── WHAT ADMITS THE MINING POOL, AND WHY ONLY NOW ────────────────────────────
#
# `docker-compose.estate.yml` puts `pool` and `pool-migrate` behind the `pool`
# profile, and its block there says a plain `up` brings up no pool because two
# variables had no honest value. Both now do. `POOL_FEE_BASIS_POINTS` was decided
# on 2026-08-09 — 1%, 100 basis points, defaulted in the compose file — and
# `POOL_LTC_PAYOUT_ADDRESS` was minted the same day through custody's operator
# route (micro-org#293) under the `pool` purpose, which exists precisely so a pool
# payout key can never become settlement's next treasury rotation candidate. It is
# set in `estate/tokens.env` beside `LTC_RPC_URL`, which is where this estate's
# chain configuration lives and the only file that is not shared with testnet.
#
# The opt-in is here rather than on a command line for the faucet's reason: a
# service brought up by a flag somebody remembered is a service the next release
# deploy silently drops. `release-deploy.sh` renders the pinned overlay from the
# service list this file produces, so a pool that is not admitted here is a pool
# that is not pinned, and an unpinned service keeps its `build:` and tries to
# compile from source on a host that has images and no checkout.
#
# LTC AND BTC, and both are measurements rather than preferences. The compose
# default is `ltc` alone, which is the right default for a host with no bitcoind;
# this estate overrides it because bitcoind reached the tip on 2026-08-10, and the
# condition the compose file wrote down on 2026-08-09 — "add `btc` when bitcoind
# reports `initialblockdownload: false`, and not before" — is now met. Measured
# 2026-08-11: height 961,967, `initialblockdownload: false`.
#
# Two consequences worth knowing before reading a `GET /v1/pool` response:
# BTC templates here carry ZERO transactions because this bitcoind runs
# `blocksonly=1` and keeps no mempool, so a found block pays the 3.125 BTC subsidy
# and forgoes fees; and BTC is refused to browsers by the service itself
# (micro-org#360), so adding it admits mining hardware over Stratum and no browser
# at all. `docker-compose.estate.yml` argues both at the pool block.
POOL_CHAINS=ltc,btc
COMPOSE_PROFILES=pool

# AND WHAT THE CONSOLE IS TOLD ABOUT THAT (micro-org#406). Restating the image's
# own default, which is the thing this file otherwise refuses to do — see "WHAT
# IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* HERE" below, and it is the right rule.
#
# The exception is bought with the line above. The two facts are the same fact
# said twice: `COMPOSE_PROFILES=pool` brings the pool API up, and this tells
# `pool-web` there is one to talk to. On `testnet.env` both lines are the other
# way round, and a reader comparing the files sees one difference rather than
# having to know that a variable absent from one of them means anything.
#
# The value that DOES something is `absent`; everything else, this included,
# means "there is a pool here" and leaves the console exactly as it has always
# been. So the cost of this line is one line, and the cost of omitting it is a
# reader who finds `POOL_API_PRESENCE=absent` on testnet and has no way to tell
# whether mainnet was configured or merely forgotten.
POOL_API_PRESENCE=present

# ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* HERE ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `CF_PROJECT`, `CF_NET_PREFIX`, `CF_PORT_BASE`, `CF_GATEWAY_PORT` and
# `CF_GW_PORT_BASE` are all set by `testnet.env` and all omitted here, because
# every one of their compose defaults IS the mainnet value — project
# `cloudsforge-estate`, port base 4, gateway 9081. Restating a default is how
# two sources of truth start, and the next person to change the default would
# have to know to change it in two places.
#
# THE CHAIN IS THE ONE EXCEPTION WORTH NAMING. `CF_EMBER_NETWORK` and
# `EMBER_RPC_URL` are set explicitly by `testnet.env` because the chain was the
# part isolation missed entirely. Mainnet's defaults are correct
# (`CF_EMBER_NETWORK=mainnet`, chain id 7411) and are asserted on every run by
# `scripts/verify-chain-backing.sh` rather than restated here — an assertion
# that fails is worth more than a line that agrees.
#
# ── CREDENTIALS ARE NOT HERE EITHER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT ─────────────
#
# `--env-file` REPLACES the default `.env`; it does not add to it. Passing this
# file ALONE would therefore drop `estate/tokens.env` — the symlinked `.env` —
# and every `*_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL` in the estate would interpolate to empty.
# That is not a hypothetical: it is exactly how testnet came to have no
# credentials at all (#158), and removing an empty-defaulting placeholder is
# what took eleven testnet services down behind `service_completed_successfully`
# (#116).
#
# The flag is REPEATABLE and repeated flags MERGE in order. Always pass BOTH:
#
#   --env-file compose/mainnet.env --env-file compose/estate/tokens.env
#
# Tokens second, so that file wins on any key the two ever share.

# ── THE BACKUP RUNNER'S no-new-privileges, TURNED OFF BECAUSE THIS HOST CANNOT
#    HONOUR IT (micro-org#329) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `docker-compose.backup.yml` asks for `no-new-privileges:true` and is right to:
# that container holds a superuser DSN for the whole cluster and reads the
# custody vault. Docker on the estate host is the CANONICAL SNAP (29.3.1, rev
# 3505), and on that build the flag makes a container fail to exec anything at
# all. Measured on the host 2026-08-10 with no compose file involved:
#
#   docker run --rm --security-opt no-new-privileges:true alpine:3.20 echo ok
#     -> exec /bin/echo: operation not permitted
#
# It is not this image, not this service and not AppArmor — `apparmor=unconfined`
# changes nothing, and a plain `docker run` of the same image works. The backup
# runner was simply the first container in the estate to ask for the flag.
#
# SET HERE AND NOT IN THE COMPOSE DEFAULT. The default stays `true`, so a host
# that can comply does; this line is one host declaring that it cannot, in the
# file where this host's other peculiarities already live. It is a HOST fact
# rather than an environment one, so `testnet.env` carries the same line — both
# estates run on this machine, and both will stop needing it on the same day
# docker here stops being a snap.
CF_BACKUP_SECURITY_OPT=no-new-privileges:false
